  "answer": "The grep shows `shift` at the very end (line 1602 of 1602). Let me inspect that region with a line-window read, plus the test expectations.\nFound it: line 1602 returns `table[key + 1]` while the test expects `shift([10,20,30], 0) === 10`. Now I'll fix only that lookup expression using the fingerprint from my read.\nFix applied. Now let me check the test runner and run the tests.\nTests use `bun:test`. Running the test suite now.\nFixed and verified. Summary:\n\n**The bug** — `src/registry.ts` (line 1602, the file's final line) contained an off-by-one lookup:\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key + 1] }\n```\nSo `shift([10, 20, 30], 0)` returned `20` instead of `10`, and `shift([10, 20, 30], 2)` returned `undefined`.\n\n**The fix** — a single expression edit, applied via a fingerprint-checked conditional replacement:\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key] }\n```\n\n**Scope check** — I inspected the file with line-window reads (`grep` located `shift` at line 1602, then `read_file` with `offset: 1590, limit: 13` gave the context and SHA fingerprint). Only that one lookup expression changed; all 1599 `handler*` exports and everything else are untouched, and the test file/package.json were not modified.\n\n**Test result** — `bun test test/oracle.test.ts`:\n```\n(pass) lookup at the key\n 1 pass, 0 fail, 3 expect() calls\n```\nAll assertions pass: `shift([10,20,30], 0) === 10`, `shift([10,20,30], 2) === 30`, and `handler7(1) === 8`."
